<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hecate</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hekate</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hecate was a Greek goddess associated with crossroads, entrances, night, magic and the moon, often shown with torches or in triple form. She was widely worshipped, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> honoured her as a protector of the city. A later legend held that she revealed a Macedonian night attack on Byzantium in 340 BCE, explaining the crescent on the city's coins.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hecate.jpg" alt="Hecate" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zde · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Relief_triplicate_Hekate_marble,_Hadrian_clasicism,_Prague_Kinsky,_NM-H10_4742,_140995.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Hecate's earliest appearance in literature is in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a>'s Theogony in the 8th century BCE, where she is described as a goddess of great honour with domains in sky, earth and sea. Evidence suggests she originated among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carians">Carians</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, where most names invoking her, such as Hecataeus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hecatomnus">Hecatomnus</a>, are attested, and where her unrivalled cult site stood at Lagina. She may derive from local sun goddesses. The earliest evidence of her cult comes from Selinunte in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, where she had a temple in the 6th and 5th centuries BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Hecate was associated with borders, city walls, doorways and crossroads, and so with realms beyond the world of the living. Her cult titles reflect this role: Enodia, on the way; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/propylaea">Propylaia</a>, before the gate; Triodia, who frequents crossroads; and Klêidouchos, holding the keys. She was expected to avert harmful spirits from the house or city she guarded, and so could also refuse to avert them. As holder of the keys between realms she is recognised as a chthonic goddess. In the 3rd century BCE Theocritus describes her unlocking the gates of death, and she appears with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermes">Hermes</a> guiding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persephone">Persephone</a> back from the underworld.</p><p>By the 1st century CE her chthonic and nocturnal character had made her a goddess heavily associated with witchcraft; in Lucan's Pharsalia the witch Erichtho invokes her. She was also tied to plant lore, medicines and poisons, and Apollonius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> says Medea was taught medicine by her. Dogs were among her most important attributes, and garlic was a favoured offering. Her lunar role rests on late evidence: no artwork before the Roman period connects her to the Moon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Hecate's cult became established in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> about 430 BCE, when the sculptor Alcamenes made the earliest known triple-formed statue for her new temple. Home shrines often took the form of a Hekataion, a carving of a triple Hecate facing three directions around a central pillar, and larger ones stood at public crossroads, such as on the road to the Acropolis.</p><p>Athenians honoured her at the Deipnon, a meal served to Hecate and the restless dead once a lunar month at the dark moon. It was set out at a small shrine by the front door, since the street and the doorway form a crossroads where she dwelled, and might include cake or bread, fish, eggs and honey. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> often knew her as Trivia, an epithet shared with Diana, and in the Roman era she was closely identified with Diana and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artemis">Artemis</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hecate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its defence against Philip II became a celebrated episode, and a later legend connected the crescent on the city's coins with the goddess <b>Hecate</b>, said to have lit the night sky to reveal a Macedonian attack, though the story is not attested in contemporary sources.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
